import json,pathlib
r='frontier-40-geometry-braids-rep-27';p=pathlib.Path('research')/f'{r}-batch-25.pages.json';a=json.loads(p.read_text());src={'title':'The Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, Lemma 54.5.3 and Situation 54.7.1 (complete arguments read and refined)','url':'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/resolve.pdf'}
def row(id,title,deps,statement,strategy):return dict(id=id,kind='lemma',title=title,deps=deps,statement=statement,strategy=strategy,design_row='AG-BIR-1',local_addition=True,provenance={'statement':'literature-derived','proof':'ai-altered'},sources={'references':[src]},dependency_level=0)
h1=row('lem-local-normal-surface-modification-dimension-and-projective-cohomology','Dimension and cohomology of local normal surface modifications',['def-axiom-of-choice','def-dependent-choice','def-normal-surface-modification-and-normalized-point-blowup','lem-surface-modification-isomorphism-in-codimension-one','lem-cm-local-codimension-and-regular-quotient-ext-concentration','lem-normal-domain-implies-s-two','cor-flat-local-depth-additivity','cor-field-finite-type-over-a-field-is-a-finite-extension','thm-proper-pushforward-coherent','thm-serre-vanishing','lem-eventual-global-generation-coherent-twists','thm-cech-computes-qc-cohomology-separated-scheme-affine-cover','thm-integrality-and-finite-module-equivalences'],r'''Assume AC and DC. Let $(A,\mathfrak m)$ be a normal Noetherian local domain of dimension two and $f:X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ an integral modification. Then $X$ has dimension two, all closed points have local dimension two, $f$ is an isomorphism off the closed point, $f_*\mathcal O_X=\mathcal O_{\operatorname{Spec}A}$, and its special fibre has dimension at most one. If $X$ is projective over $A$, it has a cover by two affine opens, hence $H^q(X,F)=0$ for $q>1$ for every quasi-coherent $F$. In particular $H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)$ has finite length over $A$.''',r'''Normality and dimension two make $A$ CM. At a closed point $x$ of $X$, properness puts it on the closed fibre with finite residue extension. On an affine chart write its domain algebra $C=A[t_1,\ldots,t_N]/I$. The polynomial local ring at $x$ is CM of dimension $N+2$: flat-local depth additivity gives depth $2+N$, and lifting parameters from $A$ and the polynomial closed fibre gives dimension at most $2+N$, while depth is at most dimension. The prime $I$ avoids nonzero $A$-elements; localizing at those elements identifies chains below it with those below a maximal prime of $\operatorname{Frac}(A)[t]$ whose residue field is the common function field. Thus its height is $N$. The CM local codimension formula makes $\dim\mathcal O_{X,x}=2$. All points specialize to closed points, so all local dimensions are at most two; the scheme dimension is two. The codimension-one modification helper gives the isomorphism off $\mathfrak m$. Every affine target open has finite structure-sheaf pushforward by proper coherent finiteness. Its algebra embeds into the common function field and is integral over the normal target ring, hence equals that ring. A component of the special fibre of dimension two would be the whole integral surface, contradicting the generic isomorphism, so its dimension is at most one. For projective $X$, choose an ample embedding bundle $L$. Pick one closed point on each one-dimensional component of the fibre and a sufficiently high twist section nonzero at each: restriction to these finitely many points is onto by Serre vanishing for their ideal. Its zero set on the fibre is finite. Raise this section to a high power and choose a second section of the same twist nonzero at every point of that finite zero set, again by the finite restriction sequence and Serre vanishing. Their common-zero locus is proper over the local base and misses the closed fibre, hence is empty. Include these sections in a finite generating system of that very ample twist (the original embedding sections and its Veronese powers suffice); their nonvanishing opens are closed in affine charts of projective space, so affine. They cover $X$; their intersection is affine by separatedness, so the Čech complex has length one. Proper coherent finiteness and the isomorphism off $\mathfrak m$ make $H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)$ finite supported at the maximal ideal, hence finite length. If the fibre is zero-dimensional the same argument uses any first section nonzero on its finite set; no positive-dimensional component is assumed.''')
h2=row('lem-normalized-point-blowups-dominate-local-normal-surface-modifications','Normalized point blowups dominate local normal surface modifications',['def-axiom-of-choice','def-dependent-choice','def-normal-surface-modification-and-normalized-point-blowup',h1['id'],'lem-surface-modification-isomorphism-in-codimension-one','lem-surface-finite-type-normalization-finite','thm-one-dimensional-regular-local-rings-are-dvrs','lem-normal-domain-implies-r-one','thm-blowup-projective','thm-pullback-center-ideal-invertible','lem-finite-over-projective-noetherian-affine-base-is-projective','thm-dimension-formula-for-affine-domains'],r'''Assume AC and DC. Let $A$ be a normal two-dimensional Noetherian local domain essentially of finite type over a field or a complete equicharacteristic Noetherian local ring. Let $S$ be a normal integral modification of $\operatorname{Spec}A$, and let $Y\to S$ be an integral modification with normal $Y$. There is a finite sequence of proper normalized point blowups $S_n\to\cdots\to S_0=S$, with centers above the finite set where $Y\to S$ fails to be an isomorphism, such that $S_n$ dominates $Y$. If $S$ is projective over $A$, so is $S_n$. For regular $S$, all these are ordinary regular point blowups.''',r'''All relevant normalizations are finite by the finite-type normalization helper, including after localization. The dimension/cohomology helper makes every integral modification over $A$ two-dimensional. The codimension-one helper makes $Y\to S$ an isomorphism off finitely many closed points. Its contracted curves form a finite set: each is a one-dimensional component of a fibre over that finite set. Induct on their number. If it is zero, every fibre is finite and the codimension-one helper gives an isomorphism. Otherwise choose a contracted curve $C$ over $x$. Its generic local ring is a DVR, and its residue field is the function field of the proper integral curve $C/\kappa(x)$, of transcendence degree one. Choose $u$ in this DVR with transcendental nonzero residue and write $u=a/b$ with $a,b\in\mathcal O_{S,x}$. Both have the same strictly positive valuation: valuation zero would force their residues, and hence the residue of $u$, to lie in $\kappa(x)$. Let $S'\to S$ be the normalized blowup at $x$ and let $Y'$ be the normalization of the **closure of the common generic open** in $Y\times_SS'$. Taking this dominant component avoids extraneous vertical components of the fibre product. A contracted curve of $Y'\to S'$ cannot map to a point of $Y$, for then both projections would map it to a point of their fibre product, contradicting finiteness of its normalization. It therefore maps to a contracted curve of $Y\to S$. The map $Y'\to Y$ is an isomorphism at the generic points of these curves by codimension-one isomorphism, giving an injection of the new contracted-curve set into the old one. If the chosen $C$ persists over $x'$, the invertible pullback of $\mathfrak m_x$ has a local generator $d\in\mathfrak m_{S',x'}$ and $a=da'$, $b=db'$. The same residue ratio $u=a'/b'$ now has common valuation $v_C(a')=v_C(a)-v_C(d)$, strictly smaller. Repeat at its image as long as it remains contracted. The positive integer cannot decrease indefinitely, so after finitely many steps at least this curve disappears and the finite curve count strictly decreases. Induction ends in a normal model with no contracted curves, hence an isomorphism to its dominant transform of $Y$, giving the required map to $Y$. Blowups are projective; finite normalizations over a projective Noetherian affine-base model are projective by the explicit finite-projective helper, so the sequence stays projective when $S$ is. For regular $S$ a point blowup stays regular by the published regular-point blowup supplier, hence its normalization is the identity, and this remains true inductively.''')
h2['deps'].append('thm-blowup-regular-surface-closed-point-regular')
for h in [h1,h2]:
 idx=next(i for i,x in enumerate(a[0]['items']) if x['id']=='thm-resolution-of-normal-surface-singularities');a[0]['items'].insert(idx,h)
